{"conjecture": "Long-running systems accumulate an implicit constraint graph—a web of unstated dependencies between components that were never intended to interact. Each locally correct change optimizes within this graph but never prunes it, so the cost of a change is proportional to the number of implicit constraints it must preserve. The system becomes harder to change not because any individual change was wrong, but because the constraint graph grows monotonically while the code to manage it does not."}
